{"name":"Flax Fibre","hex":"#e0d68e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flax","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant of the genus Linum, especially Linum usitatissimum, which has a single, slender stalk, about a foot and a half high, with blue flowers. Also known as linseed, especially when referring to the seeds.","The fibers of Linum usitatissimum, grown to make linen and related textiles."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flax"},{"term":"fibre","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A single piece of a given material, elongated and roughly round in cross-section, often twisted with other fibres to form thread.","Material in the form of fibres."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fibre"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flax","blocks":[{"text":"Flax, also known as common flax or line, is a flowering plant, Linum usitatissimum, in the family Linaceae. It is cultivated as a food and fiber crop in regions of the world with temperate climates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/K%C3%B6hler%27s_Medizinal-Pflanzen_in_naturgetreuen_Abbildungen_mit_kurz_erl%C3%A4uterndem_Texte_%28Plate_16%29_BHL303594.jpg/330px-K%C3%B6hler%27s_Medizinal-Pflanzen_in_naturgetreuen_Abbildungen_mit_kurz_erl%C3%A4uterndem_Texte_%28Plate_16%29_BHL303594.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flax","wikidata":"Q45108"},{"title":"Fiber","blocks":[{"text":"Fiber is a natural or artificial substance that is significantly longer than it is wide. Fibers are often used in the manufacture of other materials. The strongest engineering materials often incorporate fibers, for example carbon fiber and ultra-high-molecular-weight polyethylene."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/PhilFIDA_natural_fibersA.jpg/330px-PhilFIDA_natural_fibersA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"